    {
      "title": "Fifteen elephant deaths at Kenya park may be due to cyanide poisoning",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/30/elephant-deaths-kenya-park-cyanide-poisoning-wildlife",
      "description": "<p>Analysis of samples collected from carcasses found traces of toxic chemical compound, says Kenya Wildlife Service</p><p>The recent deaths of 15 elephants near a park in southern Kenya in the past month were likely caused by the animals ingesting pesticides used on nearby tomato farms, the Kenya Wildlife Service (KWS) has said.</p><p>Erustus Kanga, the agency’s director general, told Citizen TV that analysis of samples collected from the carcasses had found traces of cyanide, a chemical compound used in farming as a predator control poison and a pest fumigant, which has <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/oct/26/22-more-elephants-poisoned-cyanide-zimbabwe-reserve\">also been used by poachers.</a></p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/30/elephant-deaths-kenya-park-cyanide-poisoning-wildlife\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-07-30T12:46:50"
